   "body": "**Adversarial review (Opus 5.5, round 2): REQUEST CHANGES**, head 409e7a1\n\n1. **Medium**, `App/SwiftHFRTests/Composer/ComposerLayoutHostedTests.swift:121`. `#expect(layout.cardHeight <= room)` can fail even when the fix is correct. `cardHeight` is rounded up (`ComposerHeight.swift:134`, `.rounded(.up)`). With the text past the cap, it adds back up to `availableHeight`, and that room is not rounded: it is `874 - topInset - 44 - 336 - 3 * line`, where `line` is a measured `Text` height. Scenario: if `line` is not a whole multiple of 1/3, or if the subtract-then-add round trip leaves `availableHeight + 1 ulp`, the check compares `ceil(room)` with `room` and fails on correct code. The line also proves nothing. The probe now reports `layoutWithChips`, so the line holds by construction with or without the view change: only line 120 catches the regression. Fix: replace it with a check on the drawn card, `abs(card.height - layout.textCardHeight) < 1`, as `theControlRowSits…` does at line 105. With the change reverted, that check also goes red, because the drawn card is 66 pt taller.\n2. **Low**, `ComposerLayoutHostedTests.swift:111`. On an iPad, `try #require(isPhone, …)` records a failure instead of skipping. The sibling tests use `guard isPhone else { return }`. CI picks an iPhone today. Fix: use `guard`, or the `.enabled(if:)` trait.\n\nThe rest holds: line 120 goes red when the change is reverted; the test cannot pass vacuously (a nil room fails the `#require` at line 118); the chip is shown; the other `host(...)` callers are unchanged; compile looks valid; `swiftlint` clean on the 4 files. Not built or run (Linux).\n\nRound 2 of 3.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
